<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=fish-hook">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fish hook</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as fishhook</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A fish hook is a hook used to catch fish by piercing the inside of the mouth, attached to a line and baited. The world's oldest fish hooks, made from sea snail shells, were found in Sakitari Cave on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa Island</a> and are between 22,380 and 22,770 years old. They are older than hooks from Jerimalai cave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a>, dated 23,000 to 16,000 years old, and from New Ireland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>, 20,000 to 18,000 years old. Hooks have been made of wood, bone, horn, shell, stone, bronze and iron, and quality <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> hooks appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 17th century, when hook making became a task for specialists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fish-hook.jpg" alt="Fish hook" width="330" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: daniel jaeger · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fishing_hooks_on_white_surface.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bone was worked into harpoons, <b>fish hooks</b>, pendants and other ornaments, and ostrich-shell containers have been found in the Negev.</blockquote><a href="/culture/natufians">Natufians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>about 22,500 years ago</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Sakitari Cave, Okinawa Island, Japan</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The earliest prehistoric tackle is the gorge, a double-pointed stick tied to a line, which anchored in the fish's gullet and worked similarly to a hook.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=fish-hook">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
